This is where scientists argue most. Neanderthals cared for injured family members. One man found in Shanidar Cave in Iraq had a withered arm and was partly blind, yet he lived for years, so others must have helped him. Some sites seem to show deliberate burials, though not everyone agrees.

There are also hints of decoration: shells coloured with red pigment, and eagle claws that may have been worn as jewellery. In 2018, researchers dated red markings in Spanish caves to more than 64,000 years ago, before modern humans arrived in Europe. If the dates are right, Neanderthals made [[@cave-art|cave paintings]]. Other experts doubt the dates.

{{story:dna}}

Zircon makes a perfect clock. When a zircon [[crystal|crystal]] forms, it traps a little uranium but almost no lead. Over time, the uranium slowly changes into lead at a known, steady rate. By measuring how much lead has built up, scientists can work out how long ago the crystal formed. That steady rate is measured by the [[@half-life|half-life]] of uranium.

Zircons are also tough. They survive being worn down, washed along by rivers and buried again. Some Jack Hills zircons hold chemical clues that water was already around when they formed. That suggests early Earth cooled and grew oceans sooner than scientists once believed.

{{story:meteorites}}

GPS has to allow for [[@einstein|Albert Einstein]]'s theories of relativity. Clocks moving fast tick slightly slower, and clocks in weaker gravity tick slightly faster. GPS satellites are moving at about 14,000 km/h, but they are also far from Earth's gravity.

{{story:einstein}}

Add the two effects together, and the satellites' clocks gain about 38 millionths of a second every day compared with clocks on the ground. That sounds like nothing. But light travels about 11 km in that time. If engineers didn't adjust the satellite clocks, GPS would drift by about 10 km a day.

The story starts in 1946 at the General Electric lab in New York State. Vincent Schaefer was studying how ice forms in clouds, using a home freezer filled with his own breath to make a mini cloud. One hot July day, he dropped dry ice into it to cool it down. Instantly, the cloud turned into thousands of sparkling ice crystals.

In November 1946, Schaefer flew a small plane over Mount Greylock in Massachusetts and dropped dry ice into a cloud. Snow fell from the cloud. His colleague Bernard Vonnegut then found that silver iodide, whose crystals are shaped much like ice, worked even better. Bernard's younger brother was the famous novelist Kurt Vonnegut, who later wrote a story about a substance that froze the world.

{{story:clouds-rain}}

Before tractors, almost every farm in India depended on a pair of bullocks. They pulled ploughs through the soil, turned wheels that lifted water from wells, crushed sugarcane and carried crops to market in carts.

Dung was just as useful. Pressed into flat cakes and dried in the sun, it burns slowly in a village stove. Mixed with water, it makes a smooth, clean floor coating. In [[@biogas|biogas plants]], dung is left to rot in a closed tank, giving off a gas that can be used for cooking. What's left over is good fertiliser.

{{story:white-revolution}}

Flies are some of the most important pollinators after bees, especially in cooler places. Tiny midges pollinate cocoa flowers, the source of [[@chocolate-tree|chocolate]]. Beetles were probably among the first pollinators, visiting flowers over 100 million years ago. Every kind of fig depends on its own tiny [[@fig-wasps|fig wasp]].

Birds pollinate too. In the Americas, [[@hummingbirds|hummingbirds]] hover at red, tube-shaped flowers. In India, sunbirds do a similar job. Bats visit flowers that open at night, such as those of the agave in Mexico, which is used to make tequila, and the durian in Southeast Asia.

{{story:hummingbirds}}

Real steam is invisible. Look closely at the spout of a boiling kettle and you'll see a small clear gap just above it. The white cloud starts a little further away. That cloud is made of tiny droplets of liquid water, formed when the hot vapour hits cooler air and turns back into liquid.

Clouds in the sky form the same way, when water vapour cools high above the ground. The [[@water-cycle|water cycle]] is really one enormous, slow version of a kettle: the Sun heats the sea, vapour rises, and it cools into clouds and rain.

{{story:steam-engine}}

Two main styles grew up. In the north, the Nagara style has a curved tower called a *shikhara*, which rises like a mountain peak and is often topped with a ribbed stone disc. The temples at [[@madhya-pradesh|Khajuraho]] in Madhya Pradesh are famous examples.

In the south, the Dravida style has a stepped, pyramid-like tower called a *vimana* over the shrine. Later southern temples added [[@tamil-nadu-temples|huge gateway towers]] called *gopurams*, covered in colourful figures, which can be taller than the main shrine. There were many regional blends in between, including in [[@odisha|Odisha]], home of [[@konark|Konark]].

{{story:lever-lift}}

The syahi is made by mixing iron filings, soot or charcoal, flour and a sticky gum into a paste. A craftsman spreads it on in many thin layers, polishing each one with a smooth stone. It can take days.

In 1920, the physicist C.V. Raman and a colleague studied Indian drums like the tabla and mridangam. Most drums vibrate in a messy way that sounds like a thump. Raman showed that the weighted centre makes the extra vibrations line up neatly, like the notes on a string. That is why a tabla can be tuned to match a singer.

{{story:sound-travel}}

The Elements is divided into 13 parts, called books. Euclid began with definitions, such as "a point is that which has no part". Then he stated five postulates, things he asked readers to accept without proof. One says you can always draw a straight line between two points. Another says you can draw a circle with any centre and any size.

From these, he proved everything else, one step at a time. The very first result shows how to draw a triangle with three equal sides using just two circles. Later books cover [[@shapes-angles|shapes and angles]], areas, ratios, solid shapes and the properties of numbers.

{{story:algorithms}}

Say you want to move four discs. Here's the secret. First, move the top three discs out of the way to the spare peg. Then move the big bottom disc to the target peg. Finally, move the three discs back on top of it.

But how do you move three? The same way: move two out of the way, shift the big one, move the two back. Each puzzle contains a smaller copy of itself. Computer scientists call this recursion, and the Tower of Hanoi is one of the first examples taught in [[@programming|programming]] classes, because the whole solution fits in a few lines of code.

{{story:algorithms}}

In 1956, Sorcar appeared on the BBC programme *Panorama* in London. He hypnotised his assistant, laid her on a table and sawed her in half with a buzzing circular saw. Then the presenter stepped in and ended the programme, as it had run out of time.

Viewers were left with the image of a woman cut in two. Many rang the BBC in a panic. The next day, newspapers carried the story, and Sorcar's shows in London sold out. Many people think the abrupt ending was planned. Either way, it was a lesson in how a cliffhanger can grab attention.

{{story:optical-illusions}}

In the late 1800s and early 1900s, American companies began selling ready-to-eat [[@cereal-history|breakfast cereals]]. Firms such as Kellogg's promoted breakfast heavily, and advertisements told families it was the most important meal for good health.

Breakfast was already important to many people, but advertising helped turn it into a rule. Some of the early research on breakfast was even paid for by cereal makers. That doesn't make it wrong, but it is a reason to read it carefully. Our article on [[@statistics-lies|how numbers can mislead]] explains why.

{{story:statistics-lies}}

When the United States entered the Second World War, Hopper wanted to serve. At first the Navy said she was too old, too light and too valuable as a maths teacher. She kept trying, and in 1943 she joined the Navy's reserve force for women.

She was sent to Harvard University to work on the Mark I, one of the [[@first-computers|first computers]] in the United States. It was about 15 metres long, as long as a city bus, and weighed about 4.5 tonnes. It clattered as it worked. Hopper and her team wrote programs for it to solve maths problems for the war, including calculations for the atomic bomb project.

{{story:women-maths}}

In the 1950s, India was planning its economy and needed numbers: how much grain was grown, how many people were poor, how factories were doing. Mahalanobis had started a huge survey to collect such facts, a cousin of the [[@census|census]]. Working them out by hand took armies of clerks.

So in 1956 his institute brought in the HEC-2M from Britain. In 1959 a second machine, the URAL, arrived from the Soviet Union. The HEC-2M read its instructions from punched cards, stiff paper cards in which the pattern of holes stood for numbers and commands.

{{story:census}}

Scientists come because Antarctica holds records that exist nowhere else. Ice cores, long tubes of ice drilled out of the ice sheet, trap tiny bubbles of ancient air. They show how the climate changed through the [[@ice-ages|ice ages]] over hundreds of thousands of years.

Others watch the [[@aurora|aurora]] and the sky, measure the ozone layer, study rocks and track how fast the ice is melting into the sea. Biologists count seals, krill and [[@penguins-cold|penguins]]. The clean, dry air is also good for telescopes, which is one reason the United States runs a station right at the South Pole.

{{story:penguins-cold}}

By about 1050 BCE, the Phoenicians, a seafaring people on the coast of what is now Lebanon, were using a neat alphabet of 22 letters. All of them were consonants. Readers filled in the vowels from the sense of the sentence, the way you might read "brd" as "bird" or "bread" depending on what came before.

Phoenician ships traded all around the Mediterranean, and their alphabet travelled with them. Through a related script called Aramaic, it became the parent of the Hebrew and Arabic scripts. Sailing west, it reached Greece.

{{story:writing-began}}

The Scripps National Spelling Bee began in 1925 with nine spellers. The first champion, Frank Neuhauser, 11, won with "gladiolus", a kind of flower. Today, more than 200 spellers aged 15 and under reach the finals near Washington DC, after winning school and regional bees. The bee is shown live on American television.

The final rounds can be strange. In 2026, two spellers were still level when time ran out, so the bee used a spell-off. Shrey Parikh, 14, from California, spelled 32 words in 90 seconds, beating the old record. His winning word was "cashaw", a type of plant.

{{story:memory}}
